for years zanav national park in  mozambique was called to the silent park  after being devastated during a 15-year  civil war which began in the late 1970s  it was this eerie feeling of just dead  quietness there was not a bird not an  insect and not an animal in sight south  african organization peace parks  partnered with the mozambican government  to bring back wild animals and restore  the park to its former glory  it's about 400 000 hectares big trees  animals paradise  so far they've brought in lions and  leopards zebra and antelope and last but  definitely not least it's these guys  turn  black and white groaner will be the last  historical species that are yet to be  reintroduced into into xanav and  obviously by bringing the biggest  species it gets the smaller species  going the birds and the beetles  it's the first time rhinos have been in  sonar for 40 years and moving them from  south africa was no small undertaking  the organizers say it was a difficult  and expensive operation transferring  animals that can weigh up to two and a  half thousand kilograms  nineteen white rhinos made the trip  that's hoped to be the first of several  to relocate about 40 black and white  rhinos to their new home  having the political  will to reintroduce rhinos and all the  complications that come with it it's  very significant in what showing what  mozambique is prepared to do for  conservation it's a 60 to 70 hour  overland journey the longest road  transfer of rhinos ever completed the  animals were first taken across the  south african border to maputo national  park in mozambique they had two days  there to rest and rehydrate before being  reloaded and taken the rest of the way  to zanav  we have actually proved that this is  sort of a new way that we can now move  rhinos great distances across the  continent it's never been done before  and with halfway houses in the right  places we can actually restock protected  areas in in other countries a long way  away from from from us in south africa  there are only about 5 000 black rhino  left and about 18 000 white rhino to  protect them from poachers they're being  kept in a specially built high security  sanctuary within the park it's an  incredible feeling to be part of this  and to witness this this moment is  actually quite emotional  and the population has already expanded  with the arrival of this little one  alexia o'brien al jazeera
 

